<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Finding the mistake when the second derivative is zero</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Kamau studies the curve \(y = {@f@}\). He writes:</p><p style="margin-left:2em;">\(\dfrac{dy}{dx} = {@df@}\), which is \(0\) at \(x = {@h@}\).<br>\(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} = {@d2@}\), which is also \(0\) at \(x = {@h@}\).<br>So the stationary point \(({@h@}, {@k@})\) is a point of inflection.</p><p>(a) Find \(\dfrac{dy}{dx}\) at \(x = {@h-1@}\) and at \(x = {@h+1@}\).</p><div>At \(x = {@h-1@}\): [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><div>At \(x = {@h+1@}\): [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(b) The stationary point at \(x = {@h@}\) is a [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p><p>(c) What is wrong with Kamau's reasoning?</p><div>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>When \(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 0\) at a stationary point the test is silent, not a verdict. For example, \(y = x^4\) and \(y = x^3\) both have \(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 0\) at \(x = 0\): the first has a minimum point there and the second a point of inflection. So test the gradient either side.</p><p>(a) At \(x = {@h-1@}\), \(\dfrac{dy}{dx} = {@gL@}\); at \(x = {@h+1@}\), \(\dfrac{dy}{dx} = {@gR@}\).</p><p>(b) The gradient is [[if test="is(gL>0)"]]positive[[else]]negative[[/if]] then [[if test="is(gR>0)"]]positive[[else]]negative[[/if]], so the stationary point is a [[if test="is(nt=1)"]]maximum point[[elif test="is(nt=2)"]]minimum point[[else]]point of inflection[[/if]].</p><p>(c) Kamau's calculations are right, but a zero second derivative gives no conclusion. He should have tested the gradient either side of \(x = {@h@}\).[[if test="is(nt=3)"]] Here his conclusion happens to be right, but his reason does not show it.[[/if]]</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
